 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "My indesit washing machine fills with water and drains but the drum just doesn't go round at all. Its an IWC something. Had it about 9 years. Is it worth fixing?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "At nine years old, a washing machine is near the end of its typical lifespan. Whether it is worth repairing depends on whether the fault is a simple, inexpensive part like a belt or motor brush, rather than a major component like the motor itself. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the water-level sensing or the control side. With the machine switched off does the drum turn freely by hand, and is all the water gone before it tries to spin?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "With the power off the drum turns freely by hand and quietly, no scraping or rumble. And yes, the water all drains away before it gets to the point where it should be spinning."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the drive belt. Let's see whether the machine itself can spin: take the washing out and run a spin-only programme with the drum empty. Does it spin up properly when it's empty?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I ran it empty as you suggested and the drum still doesn't turn at all in spin. I can hear the relays clicking but there's no motor noise. I did notice a faint hot electrical smell once a few weeks ago, though not since."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nA burning or hot-plastic smell, smoke, scorching or melting can mean an electrical fault or a fire risk. Stop using it, switch it off and unplug it (or turn it off at the fuse box), and don't run it again — not even as a test — until it has been checked by a qualified engineer. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
